- How to reconstruct efficiently with O(k) space?

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem evaluates understanding of weighted interval scheduling with a cardinality constraint—a fundamental DP pattern in scheduling and resource allocation. It also tests the ability to reconstruct the actual solution, not just compute the optimal value.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with an exact cardinality constraint
- Precomputing p(i) via binary search after sorting intervals by their right endpoint
- Two-dimensional DP table indexed by interval position and number selected
- Backtracking through the DP table to recover the chosen intervals

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueWithKIntervals(intervals, A, k) {
    intervals.sort((a, b) => a.r - b.r);
    const n = intervals.length;
    const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const selected = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(false));

    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        const [l, r] = [intervals[i-1].l, intervals[i-1].r];
        const v = intervals[i-1].value;
        const p = findPrev(intervals, i-1);

        for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
            if (dp[p][t-1] + v > dp[i-1][t]) {
                dp[i][t] = dp[p][t-1] + v;
                selected[i][t] = true;
            } else {
                dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
            }
        }
    }

    const result = [];
    let i = n, t = k;
    while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
        if (selected[i][t]) {
            result.unshift(i - 1);
            t--;
            i = p;
        } else {
            i--;
        }
    }

    return { maxValue: dp[n][k], indices: result };
}

function findPrev(intervals, idx) {
    let lo = 0, hi = idx;
    while (lo < hi) {
        const mid = Math.ceil((lo + hi) / 2);
        if (intervals[mid].r <= intervals[idx].l) lo = mid;
        else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return lo;
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- Preprocessing p(i): O(n log n) via sorting and binary search
- DP computation: O(n × k)
- Reconstruction: O(n + k)
- Space: O(n × k) for the DP table

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a two-dimensional DP with an inner precomputation and path reconstruction, a common interview pattern for text/partition problems.

## Key Concepts
- Palindromic edit-cost precomputation (costPal).
- Partition DP with exactly k segments.
- Reconstruction of cut points and time/space trade-offs.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPalPartitionCost(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const costPal = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      if (i >= j) costPal[i][j] = 0;
      else costPal[i][j] = costPal[i + 1][j - 1] + (s[i] !== s[j] ? 1 : 0);
    }
  }
  const INF = 1e9;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let x = 1; x <= n; x++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let i = t - 1; i < x; i++) {
        const val = dp[i][t - 1] + costPal[i][x - 1];
        if (val < dp[x][t]) {
          dp[x][t] = val;
          par[x][t] = i;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction can follow from par
  return { cost: dp[n][k], cuts: par };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to adapt for at most k substrings instead of exactly k?
- What optimizations reduce time to O(n^2) or enable pruning of the i-loop?

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a novel variation of LPS with deletions, forcing a 3D DP and careful reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP: i, j bounds plus remaining deletions t.
- Transitions handle match, deletion of either end, and base cases.
- Reconstruction by tracing choices from dp[0][n-1][k].
- Space optimization via rolling t or two 2D layers.

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxLPSWithDeletions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][i][0] = 1;
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (s[i] === s[j]) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], (i+1<=j-1? dp[i+1][j-1][t] : 0) + 2);
        }
        if (t > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i+1][j][t-1], dp[i][j-1][t-1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0][n-1][k] || 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(nk) space while preserving O(n^2 k) time?
- How does the solution change if deletions must be exactly k?

## Why This Problem Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming on partitioning with a non-linear cost function (block maximum). It extends beyond standard max-sum variants and evaluates careful indexing, prefix handling, and solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- **Contiguous partition DP**: State dp[i][t] represents the minimum cost for the prefix of length i using exactly t blocks
- **Transition**: Computes the running maximum for the last block while finding the optimal split point
- **Reconstruction**: Backtracking through the DP table to determine block endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumOfBlockMax(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const split = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i));
  }

  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      let maxVal = 0;
      for (let j = i - 1; j >= t - 1; j--) {
        maxVal = Math.max(maxVal, A[j]);
        const cost = dp[j][t - 1] + maxVal;
        if (cost < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = cost;
          split[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = split[i][t];
    blocks.unshift({ start: j + 1, end: i });
    i = j;
    t--;
  }

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
The task tests a non-trivial DP where block value is a segment maximum, not sum. It requires precomputing segment maxima and careful reconstruction, exposing trade-offs between time and memory.

## Key Concepts
- DP on prefixes with exact block counts
- Transition uses max(A[j..i])
- Precompute M[j][i] in O(n^2)
- Reconstruction pointers for endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumKBlocks(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const M=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ let m=-Infinity; for(let j=i;j>=0;j--){ m=Math.max(m,A[j]); M[j][i]=m; } }
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const split=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=Math.min(k,i);t++){
      for(let j=1;j<=i;j++){
        const val=dp[j-1][t-1]+M[j-1][i-1];
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; split[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best:dp[n][k]};
}
```

